{% sec 1, "What Market Data Is", "What exactly is 'market data'?", "What market data is" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think of a football match. There's the live score in your app, ticking over as goals go in. There's the full live coverage, where you see every pass. And afterwards there are the stats and the replay, which you can study as long as you like.</p>
    <p>{% gl "market data" %}Market data{% endgl %} is the same three things for a market:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Quotes</strong>: the prices people are currently willing to buy and sell at, and how much. Like seeing where every player is standing.</li>
      <li><strong>Trades</strong>: what actually happened, at what price and size, and when. Like the goals.</li>
      <li><strong>History</strong>: all of that, stored, so you can look back, measure things and test ideas.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every venue from the last note produces its own stream of quotes and trades, many times a second. Traders don't just need the data, they need to know <em>how much</em> of it they can see, <em>how fast</em> it reaches them, and <em>how clean</em> it is when they look back. That's this note.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>Market data is quotes (what people would trade at), trades (what did trade) and history. What matters is how much you see, how fast, and how clean.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "How Much Can You See?", "What do the different 'levels' of data actually show?", "How much can you see?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Data comes in levels, and each one shows more of the order book. Press each level below to see what a trader with that level would see for the same share at the same moment.</p>
  </div>
