  <div class="prose">
    <p>Notice what no level shows: <strong>who</strong> the orders belong to. Even with the full book in front of you, the other side is anonymous. You see that someone wants to sell \(12{,}500\) shares at \(230.9\text{p}\). You never see that it's a pension fund.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "One Price from Many Venues", "If a share trades in twenty places, what is 'the' price?", "One price from many venues" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The last note ended with a problem: one share can trade on a dozen venues at once. So which price is the real one?</p>
    <p>In the US, the answer is the {% gl "consolidated tape" %}consolidated tape{% endgl %}. Every exchange has to send its quotes and trades to a central processor, which stitches them into one feed of every trade in every share. From it comes the {% gl "NBBO" %}national best bid and offer{% endgl %} (NBBO): the best price to sell at and the best price to buy at, across all exchanges, at that moment. US rules say trades must happen at the NBBO or better, which is how all the separate venues stay tied together.</p>
    <h3>The race to see it first</h3>
    <p>There's a catch. Collecting data from every exchange, combining it and sending it back out takes time: a fraction of a millisecond. For a person, that's nothing. For a computer, it's long enough to read a price change on one exchange and trade on another before the official feed has caught up.</p>
    <p>So the fastest firms skip the tape. They pay each exchange for a {% gl "direct feed" %}direct feed{% endgl %}, put their computers in the same buildings as the exchanges' computers, and run cables or even microwave towers between data centres in the straightest line they can. The delay between something happening and you finding out about it is called {% gl "latency" %}latency{% endgl %}, and for some firms, shaving it is the whole business.</p>
    <p>The hard limit is the speed of light. Signals in glass fibre travel at about two-thirds of the speed of light, while signals through the air travel at almost the full speed of light. That's why firms build microwave links between Chicago and New Jersey, even though fibre carries far more data. Try some real distances:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>How fast can news travel?</h3><p>The quickest a signal could possibly make each journey, in a perfectly straight line.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets route-picks" aria-label="Routes">
        <button type="button" data-route="0" aria-pressed="false">London → Frankfurt</button>
        <button type="button" data-route="1" aria-pressed="true">Chicago → New Jersey</button>
        <button type="button" data-route="2" aria-pressed="false">London → Lagos</button>
        <button type="button" data-route="3" aria-pressed="false">London → New York</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-race" viewBox="0 0 640 200" role="img" aria-label="Time for a signal to travel the route through fibre and through the air"></svg>
    <div class="legend"><span><i class="sw grey"></i>Through glass fibre</span><span><i class="sw tone"></i>Through the air (microwave or radio)</span></div>
